{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Your account{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Your account</h1>

    {# Exactly one address, and it is the visitor's own (AC-21). No roles, no password hash, no
       other user's data — the controller passes a single string, so there is nothing else here to
       render even by accident (AC-22). #}
    <p>Signed in as <span class="account-email">{{ email }}</span>.</p>

    {# A POST form, not `logout_path()`'s tokenised GET link.

       `logout_path()` would work — the firewall accepts the token from the query string — but it
       puts a live, session-valid token into a URL, where it ends up in access logs, browser history
       and `Referer` headers. Worse, it stays a GET: a link prefetch, a "link preview" bot or an
       over-eager crawler following an href in an authenticated page signs the user out, and the
       token in that href is genuinely valid, so CSRF protection does not stop it. Only the method
       does. State-changing means POST.

       No JS involved: a plain submit button is a form control, so this works with scripting off,
       which matters because this slice ships no JS at all. #}
    <form action="{{ path('app_logout') }}" method="post">
        <input type="hidden" name="_csrf_token" value="{{ csrf_token('logout') }}">
        <button type="submit">Sign out</button>
    </form>
{% endblock %}
